res weather knowledge from Aboriginal communities. Aboriginal people have always had a deep understanding of the weather and the environment. Unlike the typical four seasons, Aboriginal groups in Australia recognize many different seasons based on natural signs. For example, the Noongar people in Western Australia recognize six seasons, which are based on changes in the plants, animals, and weather around them. This site lets us learn how different Aboriginal groups read nature to understand the world around them. ";}i:2;i:4057;}i:192;a:3:{i:0;s:9:"linebreak";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4643;}i:193;a:3:{i:0;s:9:"linebreak";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4646;}i:194;a:3:{i:0;s:12:"externallink";i:1;a:2:{i:0;s:26:"http://www.bom.gov.au/iwk/";i:1;s:14:"Visit Resource";}i:2;i:4649;}i:195;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:1:" ";}i:2;i:4694;}i:196;a:3:{i:0;s:15:"tablecell_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4695;}i:197;a:3:{i:0;s:14:"tablerow_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4697;}i:198;a:3:{i:0;s:13:"tablerow_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4697;}i:199;a:3:{i:0;s:14:"tablecell_open";i:1;a:3:{i:0;i:1;i:1;N;i:2;i:1;}i:2;i:4697;}i:200;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:1:" ";}i:2;i:4699;}i:201;a:3:{i:0;s:13:"internalmedia";i:1;a:7:{i:0;s:7:":cg.png";i:1;s:0:"";i:2;N;i:3;s:3:"360";i:4;N;i:5;s:5:"cache";i:6;s:7:"details";}i:2;i:4700;}i:202;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:1:" ";}i:2;i:4716;}i:203;a:3:{i:0;s:9:"linebreak";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4717;}i:204;a:3:{i:0;s:11:"strong_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4720;}i:205;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:14:"Common Ground ";}i:2;i:4722;}i:206;a:3:{i:0;s:12:"strong_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4736;}i:207;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:1:" ";}i:2;i:4738;}i:208;a:3:{i:0;s:9:"linebreak";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:4739;}i:209;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:559:"Common Ground is a fantastic website that helps people learn about Aboriginal stories, symbols, and history. In Aboriginal culture, symbols are often used in art and have special meanings. For example, a U shape can represent a person sitting. This website also shares Aboriginal stories and histories that aren’t usually taught in regular history classes, helping us learn about Australia’s past from an Aboriginal viewpoint. Common Ground helps us understand the meanings behind symbols and shows us why stories are important for keeping culture alive. ";}i:2;i:4742;}i:210;a:3:{i:0;s:9:"linebreak";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:5301;}i:211;a:3:{i:0;s:9:"linebreak";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:5304;}i:212;a:3:{i:0;s:12:"externallink";i:1;a:2:{i:0;s:32:"https://www.commonground.org.au/";i:1;s:14:"Visit Resource";}i:2;i:5307;}i:213;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:1:" ";}i:2;i:5358;}i:214;a:3:{i:0;s:15:"tablecell_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:5359;}i:215;a:3:{i:0;s:14:"tablecell_open";i:1;a:3:{i:0;i:1;i:1;s:4:"left";i:2;i:1;}i:2;i:5359;}i:216;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:1:" ";}i:2;i:5360;}i:217;a:3:{i:0;s:13:"internalmedia";i:1;a:7:{i:0;s:9:":art2.jpg";i:1;s:0:"";i:2;N;i:3;s:3:"360";i:4;N;i:5;s:5:"cache";i:6;s:7:"details";}i:2;i:5361;}i:218;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:1:" ";}i:2;i:5379;}i:219;a:3:{i:0;s:9:"linebreak";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:5380;}i:220;a:3:{i:0;s:11:"strong_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:5383;}i:221;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:24:"Aboriginal Art Australia";}i:2;i:5385;}i:222;a:3:{i:0;s:12:"strong_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:5409;}i:223;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:1:" ";}i:2;i:5411;}i:224;a:3:{i:0;s:9:"linebreak";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:5412;}i:225;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:544:"Aboriginal Art Australia focuses on Indigenous art. Aboriginal art is famous around the world and is unique because it’s tied to the land, stories, and spirituality. This website explains different art styles like dot painting and rock art, and it shows us how each artwork tells a story about culture or history. For example, an artist like Emily Kame Kngwarreye created beautiful paintings that represented her deep connection to the land. This website helps us see art not just as decoration but as a way of expressing cultural knowledge. ";}i:2;i: